Chapter: DiagNicRing
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Kernel chapter Pci
  cites Kernel chapter Hpet
  cites Kernel chapter E1000e
  cites Dev chapter NicAsde
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Diag chapter DiagStage

 build/boot/diag/NicRingProbe.codex as a stage: NIC-4's question. Read the DD
 bit of every RX descriptor (a pure read of a ring we own), send one ARP
 request for the gateway and listen, then the reader's own control: the TX
 descriptor e1000-send-frame answered on must show DD, or the reader is
 broken and every RX bit above it means nothing. The ARP is a real request
 for 10.0.2.2 from 10.0.2.15, so a NAT bed answers it and a live switch
 ignores it harmlessly.

 THE ONE WRITE COMES FIRST NOW, and it is what 2026-08-19 cost. RDH set to 7
 and read back tells "RDH tracks reception" and "RDH is not writable here"
 apart, which IS NIC-4, and it used to run after two listens. The stage went
 grey for more than ten minutes on the I219-V and was pulled, so the whole
 flight bought nothing: the answer the sitting was for is three MMIO cycles
 and it was standing behind every wait in the stage. It is banked before
 anything that can spin (L-BANK). The write now lands on the ring nicinit
 left running rather than on one this stage had just built, which is the
 more honest place to ask whether the part accepts it.

 IT NO LONGER CALLS e1000-init. That is a second CTRL.RST, a second PHY
 reset, a second aneg wait and a second link wait on a part nicinit brought
 up two stages earlier. The rings are set up from the same primitives with
 no reset and the link is read through na-link-wait, which checks the clock
 every iteration rather than once per 4096 reads.

 WHAT THE 08-19 GLASS COULD NOT SAY, and why hpet is banked early. With a
 usable HPET every wait on that path is budgeted and the whole stage is
 about ten seconds: aneg 3 s, e1000-link-wait 5 s, two listens 1.2 s each.
 The count fallbacks are what take minutes -- aneg 1,000,000 reads measured
 at 92.89 us is 93 s, and e1000-await-link's 4,000,000 is about six minutes
 more -- and they are reached only where hpet-ticks-per-second answers zero
 or less. So the ten-minute grey row and a good clock do not fit together,
 and nothing was banked to tell us which held. The rate is in the arrival
 row now, and a stage that finds no clock says so and returns instead of
 spending seven minutes finding out.

 We say:

Section: States

  nir-states : List Text = ["frames", "quiet", "reader-broken", "no-part", "rejected", "bar-bad", "send-refused", "no-hpet"]

  nir-src-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 15]
  nir-gw-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 2]
  nir-bcast : List Integer = [255, 255, 255, 255, 255, 255]
  nir-chunk : Integer = 512
  nir-rounds : Integer = 4000

Section: Ring Reads

  nir-dd-of : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  nir-dd-of (ring) (i) = bit-and (peek-byte (ring + i * e1000-desc-size) 12) e1000-rx-status-dd

  nir-len-of : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  nir-len-of (ring) (i) =
   let d = ring + i * e1000-desc-size
   in bit-or (peek-byte d 8) (bit-shl (peek-byte d 9) 8)

  nir-dd-map : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  nir-dd-map (ring) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= e1000-rx-count then acc
   else nir-dd-map ring (i + 1) (acc & (if nir-dd-of ring i /= 0 then "1" else "0"))

  nir-dd-count : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  nir-dd-count (ring) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= e1000-rx-count then acc
   else nir-dd-count ring (i + 1) (acc + (if nir-dd-of ring i /= 0 then 1 else 0))

  nir-max-len : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  nir-max-len (ring) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= e1000-rx-count then acc
   else let l = nir-len-of ring i
   in nir-max-len ring (i + 1) (if l > acc then l else acc)

  nir-ring-line : Text, Integer -> Text
  nir-ring-line (tag) (ring) = tag & " dd=" & nir-dd-map ring 0 "" & " set=" & show (nir-dd-count ring 0 0) & " maxlen=" & show (nir-max-len ring 0 0)

  nir-tx-dd : E1000Device -> Integer
  nir-tx-dd (d) =
   let nxt = bit-and (peek-32 ((d.e-ctrl-blk) + e1000-cb-tx-next) 0) #FFFFFFFF
   in let idx = int-mod (nxt + e1000-tx-count - 1) e1000-tx-count
   in let st = peek-byte ((d.e-tx-ring) + idx * e1000-desc-size) 12
   in if bit-and st e1000-rx-status-dd /= 0 then 1 else 0

  nir-arp : List Integer -> List Integer
  nir-arp (mac) = nir-bcast & mac & [8, 6, 0, 1, 8, 0, 6, 4, 0, 1] & mac & nir-src-ip & [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0] & nir-gw-ip

  nir-poll-n : E1000Device, Integer -> E1000RecvResult
  nir-poll-n (d) (n) =
   if n <= 0 then e1000-no-frame
   else let hp = __heap-save
   in let r = e1000-poll-raw (d.e-mmio) (d.e-rx-ring) (d.e-rx-bufs) (d.e-ctrl-blk)
   in if r.r-has-frame then r
   else let rs = __heap-restore hp
   in nir-poll-n d (n - 1)

  nir-wait : E1000Device, Integer, Integer, Integer -> E1000RecvResult
  nir-wait (d) (start) (want) (rounds) =
   if rounds <= 0 then e1000-no-frame
   else let r = nir-poll-n d nir-chunk
   in if r.r-has-frame then r
   else if (hpet-ticks - start) >= want then e1000-no-frame
   else nir-wait d start want (rounds - 1)

Section: What The Part Says, Not What Our Ring Says

 NIC-4's successor question, from sitting 3: the stage returned `quiet` on the
 ASUS with link=1, sent=1, txdd=1 and received=0 ddset=0. Our own ring cannot
 tell the three explanations apart, because all three leave it empty.

 GPRC counts good frames at the MAC, before any descriptor. RNBC counts frames
 that arrived with no descriptor to put them in. Between them and the ring:

   gprc=0 rnbc=0    nothing arrived. The window was genuinely quiet and
                    received=0 is honest.
   gprc>0 ddset=0   frames arrived and we cannot see them. Writeback is
                    landing somewhere other than the ring being read, which
                    the aim row below is for.
   rnbc>0           frames arrived and were turned away for want of a
                    descriptor, so RDT is not where we think it is.

 They CLEAR ON READ, and sitting 4 turned that into an ambiguity worth
 closing. GPRC was read once, at the end, so `gprc=1` counted every window
 since power-on -- including nicinit's, two stages earlier, which builds and
 listens on a ring of its own. A frame counted there says nothing about the
 ring THIS stage built, and the reading it produced has a second explanation
 nobody could exclude from the glass: it arrived before we were looking.

 So GPRC is read TWICE. The read before the attach reports the earlier windows
 and zeroes the counter; the read at the end then counts only what arrived
 while this stage was listening on its own ring. Both are banked and the
 pre-stage count rides the QR, because "1 before, 0 during" and "0 before, 1
 during" are different findings and sitting 4 could not tell them apart.

 MPC and CRCERRS are banked alongside because they cost one read each and are
 the next questions if the three above do not settle it; codex-vm does not
 model either, so a bed reading 0 from them means "not modelled", and no arm
 asserts on them.

 THE COUNTERS ARE READ ONCE AND PASSED, never read per consumer. GPRC and RNBC
 clear on read, so a second reader gets zero and the two rows disagree by
 construction -- and the row that happened to read second would be the one
 reporting "nothing arrived". That is the same clear-on-read hazard this
 chapter already pays attention to two blocks up, one level down.

  nir-stats-text : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  nir-stats-text (gprc) (rnbc) (mpc) (crcerrs) =
   "stats gprc=" & show gprc
     & " rnbc=" & show rnbc
     & " mpc=" & show mpc
     & " crcerrs=" & show crcerrs

 Where the part is actually aimed, against where we think we put the ring. If
 RDBA does not read back as our own ring, the part is filling somebody else's
 buffers and every row above is describing the wrong memory. The comparison
 puts a physical register against our own pointer, so it means what it says
 only while the guest is identity mapped: `match=y` is not by itself proof of
 correct aim.

 RDH advances as the part consumes descriptors, and `e1000-setup-rx` writes it
 zero (`E1000e.codex`, e1000-setup-rx). This block used to say the opposite --
 that RDH is never written and the head is inherited from whatever ran before,
 which is what sitting 3's rdh=5 was read against. Because the attach really
 does zero it, a nonzero RDH at the END of the stage says the part consumed a
 descriptor from the ring it is aimed at DURING this stage's own window, which
 is a sharper question than the one the row was built to ask. The arrival row
 still reports the pre-attach RDH, and that one is inherited.

  nir-aim : Integer, Integer -> Text
  nir-aim (mmio) (ring) =
   let lo = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdbal
   in let hi = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdbah
   in let base = hi * 4294967296 + lo
   in "aim rdba=" & show base & " ours=" & show ring
     & " match=" & (if base == ring then "y" else "n")
     & " rdlen=" & show (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdlen)
     & " rdh=" & show (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdh)
     & " rdt=" & show (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdt)

Section: The Answer Row

 Receive descriptor 0, raw, all sixteen bytes. It is the one reading that does
 not depend on the aim row being meaningful, because it does not put a
 physical register against a virtual pointer.

 THE TWO HALVES OF A DESCRIPTOR ARE WRITTEN BY DIFFERENT PARTIES, and the
 discriminator lives in one of them. `e1000-build-rx-descs` writes the buffer
 address into bytes 0..7 and zeroes bytes 8..15; the hardware writes back
 length at 8..9 and status at 12. So "all sixteen bytes zero" cannot happen
 while our own ring build works, and a raw sixteen-byte test reads NONZERO on
 every flight -- our own address -- which would be read as "the part wrote our
 ring" every time, including when it never touched it.

 The writeback half is the question. `wb` counts nonzero bytes in 8..15 only:
 zero says the part never wrote back into descriptor 0, any nonzero says it
 did and we are reading the wrong field out of it. `buf` is the other half's
 own control: bytes 0..7 must still read back as the buffer address we put
 there, and a `buf=n` says something is scribbling on the ring and no row
 above it means anything.

  nir-d0-hex : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  nir-d0-hex (ring) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= e1000-desc-size then acc
   else nir-d0-hex ring (i + 1) (acc & diag-hex2 (peek-byte ring i))

  nir-d0-wb : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  nir-d0-wb (ring) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= e1000-desc-size then acc
   else nir-d0-wb ring (i + 1) (acc + (if peek-byte ring i /= 0 then 1 else 0))

  nir-d0-buf : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  nir-d0-buf (ring) (bufs) =
   bit-or (peek-32 ring 0) (bit-shl (peek-32 ring 4) 32) == bufs

 The QR summary is built from each stage's FIRST glass line and nothing else
 (`Diag.codex`, dg-summary-body), so this row is what reaches anyone when the
 bank does not survive the ladder. It is budgeted: 72 characters against the
 76 of the head row it displaces, so the summary does not gain a QR code and
 risk the cut. The keys are short for that reason and no other.

 `gp=` AND `rn=` ARE HERE BECAUSE THE STATS ROW CANNOT BE, and sitting 6 is
 what proved it. `dg-paint-result` paints glass line 0 and glass line 1, and
 line 2 only for a picture stage (`dg-stage-picture`, scene and asde); nicring
 is neither, so its `stats` row was FILE ONLY and reached the screen as the
 words `+more in bank`. The bank then died at the sink, four stages before
 this one ran, and the during-window GPRC -- the single number that separates
 "nothing arrived" from "arrived and was invisible", which is the successor
 question sitting 3 opened -- did not exist anywhere afterwards. A whole metal
 boot could not answer it.

 So the discriminator rides the row that always paints. The room comes from
 `d0w=`, which is bytes 8..15 rather than all sixteen: bytes 0..7 are the
 buffer address WE wrote, they are already reported as `buf=y/n`, and this
 chapter's own answer-row block says the writeback half is the question. The
 full sixteen stay in the file as their own line, where they cost nothing.

 Budget, single-digit counters: 3 + 6 + 5 + 6 + 6 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 16 =
 67, and 72 with `ln=0` aboard. Multi-digit counters eat the margin, as
 `pre=` always could.

  nir-answer : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  nir-answer (mmio) (ring) (bufs) (gprc0) (gprc1) (rnbc1) (ddset) (txdd) (listen) =
   let lo = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdbal
   in let hi = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdbah
   in let base = hi * 4294967296 + lo
   in "m=" & (if base == ring then "y" else "n")
     & " rdh=" & show (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdh)
     & " wb=" & show (nir-d0-wb ring 8 0)
     & " buf=" & (if nir-d0-buf ring bufs then "y" else "n")
     & " pre=" & show gprc0
     & " gp=" & show gprc1
     & " rn=" & show rnbc1
     & " dd=" & show ddset
     & " tx=" & show txdd
     & (if listen == 0 then " ln=0" else "")
     & " d0w=" & nir-d0-hex ring 8 ""

Section: The Listen Knob

 OUR OWN POLL PATH WIPES THE EVIDENCE, which is what the bed said the first
 time the arms ran against this row: `nicring=frames` with `rdh=1`, `buf=y`
 and `wb=0`. Nothing was wrong with the part. `e1000-poll-raw` takes a frame
 and recycles the descriptor it came from, so the writeback half is back to
 zero by the time the row is built, and the bed could not produce a single
 reading in which `wb` is nonzero.

 That matters beyond the arm. A control that cannot express the positive case
 is not a control, and this dump would have shipped to metal with nothing
 anywhere having shown it can read a writeback at all (L-FALSIF).

 `nicring listen=N` in DIAG.CFG. At the default 1 the stage listens as before.
 At 0 it sends the ARP and then idles the same 1.2 s WITHOUT polling, so the
 part writes into descriptor 0 and nothing consumes it -- which is the metal
 case exactly: on the ASUS nothing was consumed either, because nothing was
 ever seen. With the NAT answering, `wb` must come back nonzero there, and
 `nic-noread` in `diag-arm.ps1` is that arm.

  nir-listen : DiagCtx -> Integer
  nir-listen (c) =
   let v = diag-cfg-value c "nicring"
   in if text-starts-with v "listen=" then text-to-integer (substring v 7 (text-length v - 7)) else 1

  nir-idle : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  nir-idle (start) (want) =
   if (hpet-ticks - start) >= want then 0
   else nir-idle start want

Section: The Stage

  nir-attach : Integer -> E1000Device
  nir-attach (mmio) =
   let quiet = e1000-quiesce mmio
   in let rx-ring = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-rx-count * e1000-desc-size) 16
   in let tx-ring = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-tx-count * e1000-desc-size) 16
   in let rx-bufs = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-rx-count * e1000-buf-size) 16
   in let tx-bufs = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-tx-count * e1000-buf-size) 16
   in let ctrl-blk = e1000-alloc-aligned e1000-cb-size 8
   in let cb-clear = e1000-zero-words ctrl-blk (e1000-cb-size / 4) 0
   in let mta = e1000-clear-mta mmio 0
   in let mac-ok = e1000-mac-present mmio
   in let mac = if mac-ok then e1000-read-mac mmio else []
   in let dev = E1000Device { e-mmio = mmio, e-rx-ring = rx-ring, e-tx-ring = tx-ring, e-rx-bufs = rx-bufs, e-tx-bufs = tx-bufs, e-ctrl-blk = ctrl-blk, e-present = True, e-mac = mac, e-mac-valid = mac-ok, e-pch = 0, e-ulp = 0 }
   in let rx = e1000-setup-rx dev
   in let tx = e1000-setup-tx dev
   in dev

  nir-run-part : PciDevice, Integer -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Console] DiagResult
  nir-run-part (dv) (listen) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dv
   in let started = hpet-start
   in let hz = hpet-ticks-per-second
   in let rdh0 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdh
   in let wr = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdh 7
   in let rdh1 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdh
   in let restore = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdh rdh0
   in let writable = if rdh1 == 7 then "y" else "n"
   in let gprc0 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-gprc
   in let arrival = "arrival rctl=" & show (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rctl) & " rdh=" & show rdh0 & " rdt=" & show (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdt) & " hpet=" & show hz & " rdh-writable=" & writable & " rdh-after=" & show rdh1 & " gprc-before=" & show gprc0
   in if hz <= 0 then diag-result "no-hpet" diag-col-bad [arrival] [arrival]
   else act
    say0 <- print-line-uni "nicring entering attach (no reset)"
    let d = nir-attach mmio
    in let link = na-link-wait mmio
    in act
     say1 <- print-line-uni "nicring entering listen"
     let s0 = hpet-ticks
     in let listened = if listen == 0 then e1000-no-frame else nir-wait d s0 ((hz * 1200) / 1000) nir-rounds
     in let after-listen = nir-ring-line "after-listen" (d.e-rx-ring)
     in let sent = e1000-send-frame d (nir-arp (e1000-read-mac mmio))
     in let s1 = hpet-ticks
     in let idled = if listen == 0 then nir-idle s1 ((hz * 1200) / 1000) else 0
     in let after = if listen == 0 then e1000-no-frame else nir-wait d s1 ((hz * 1200) / 1000) nir-rounds
     in let after-send = nir-ring-line "after-send" (d.e-rx-ring)
     in let txdd = nir-tx-dd d
     in let got = if listened.r-has-frame then 1 else if after.r-has-frame then 1 else 0
     in let ddset = nir-dd-count (d.e-rx-ring) 0 0
     in let gprc1 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-gprc
     in let rnbc1 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rnbc
     in let stats = nir-stats-text gprc1 rnbc1 (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-mpc) (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-crcerrs)
     in let d0full = "d0 " & nir-d0-hex (d.e-rx-ring) 0 ""
     in let aim = nir-aim mmio (d.e-rx-ring)
     in let head = "link=" & show link & " present=" & na-yn (if d.e-present then 1 else 0) & " mac=" & na-yn (if d.e-mac-valid then 1 else 0) & " received=" & show got & " ddset=" & show ddset & " sent=" & show sent & " txdd=" & show txdd & " rdh-writable=" & writable & " rdh=" & show rdh0
     in let state = if sent /= 1 then "send-refused" else if txdd == 0 then "reader-broken" else if got == 1 then "frames" else if ddset > 0 then "frames" else "quiet"
     in let colour = if state == "frames" then diag-col-good else if state == "quiet" then diag-col-warn else diag-col-bad
     in let answer = nir-answer mmio (d.e-rx-ring) (d.e-rx-bufs) gprc0 gprc1 rnbc1 ddset txdd listen
     in diag-result state colour [answer, head, stats] [answer, arrival, head, stats, aim, after-listen, after-send, d0full]
    end
   end

  nir-run : DiagCtx -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Console] DiagResult
  nir-run (c) = act
   when e1000-find (c.dc-pci)
    is None -> diag-result "no-part" diag-col-dim ["no eligible Intel part on the bus"] ["no eligible Intel part on the bus"]
    is Just (d) -> if na-eligible d == False then diag-result "rejected" diag-col-warn [na-reject-line d] [na-reject-line d]
     else if e1000-bar-verdict (na-bar-of d) /= e1000-bar-ok then diag-result "bar-bad" diag-col-bad ["verdict=" & na-verdict-text (e1000-bar-verdict (na-bar-of d)) & ", the part was not touched"] ["verdict=" & na-verdict-text (e1000-bar-verdict (na-bar-of d)) & ", the part was not touched"]
     else nir-run-part d (nir-listen c)
  end
